eotypes in Speech and Decor Warn your friend not to make jokes about Russian drinking culture or put up signs playing into that cliché. That can make guests feel uncomfortable, misunderstood, or even pressured to live up to an expectation. Treat it like you’d treat any other cultural appreciation night: with curiosity and respect. ### 5. Manage Alcohol Quietly and Equally These are universal hosting tips that avoid targeting any one group: - Use smaller glasses for wine or spirits. - Have water, juices, and mocktails served in the same attractive glassware as alcoholic drinks, so non-drinkers don’t stand out. - Enlist a co-host or server to gently monitor consumption and discreetly slow down service if needed. If you see someone overindulging, offer food and water, or call a ride-share for them, exactly as you would for any guest regardless of background. ### 6. Remember: Guests Are Individuals The best thing your friend can do is welcome each person as an individual. Many Russians enjoy a glass of wine with dinner or a small toast on special occasions, and some don’t drink at all. The assumption that they will drink excessively is unfair and can create a weird tension. If your friend is genuinely worried about a specific guest with known drinking habits, that’s a different personal matter—but it shouldn’t be projected onto a whole culture. By framing the event around cuisine, culture, and connection, your friend will create an elegant, enjoyable evening where everyone feels comfortable. The Russian concept of *zastolye* (the art of table talk and togetherness over food) is about much more than what’s in the glass—it’s the warmth of shared company. That’s the heart of what she can bring to life.
